      "sourceFeatureExtractionItemID": null,
      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "Frankly, I'm so exhausted of this pattern I've been seeing with VRChat's development priorities as of late. The order seems to always be:\n\n - Ship a new feature for monetization,\n\n - Discover a bunch of accessibility concerns it creates, and\n\n - Ignore those and charge forward to the next monetization opportunity.\n\nI've seen it enough to recognize it as a clear pattern of priorities. It's very clear to me as a lifelong accessibility advocate that inclusion and accessibility are *extremely low priorities* for VRChat development.\n\nFrom a business standpoint, I almost get it. Folks with disabilities don't make up a big slice of the pie, so we aren't enough of a market share to really move the needle, so our concerns are sidelined as they're often more work.\n\nWhat the business critters at VRC are forgetting, though, is two important details:\n\n1) Almost everyone in the VRC communities I frequent either is, knows, or has heard of someone using the application living with some disability. VRC is a safe escape where we can craft experiences that include people who would normally be excluded from IRL versions of those same experiences, so naturally this attracts a fair number of folks living with disabilities. You can only sideline the concerns of these people for so long before them, their friends and their peers start to wonder why they're helping fund an operation that excludes people they care about.\n\nand\n\n2) Accessibility, almost universally, benefits far more people than just the folks who absolutely need, for reasons of disability, to disable a toggle or customize a feature. For every one person who requires that toggle, there are a dozen or more who simply *prefer* that experience and find it more pleasant or relaxing to them. Building in those additional settings helps more and more people feel \"at home\" in your user interface, with very few (if any) downsides.\n\nI am not fundamentally opposed in any way to the idea of VRC monetizing its platform so it can keep the lights on, recruit and retain quality staff, and build out new features, but I strongly oppose any notion that in order to do that, one must sacrifice inclusion and accessibility. It is absolutely not necessary to throw people like me under the bus to achieve your goals, and I think the fact that this continues to happen with these newly rolled-out features is a big part of why it's so difficult for me and my peers to truly feel excited about anything new coming out of VRC.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2026-07-22T23:44:09.613Z",
